The latest headless test revealed that my tool‑call parsing is broken. The raw model output contains tool markers like:

```
<<<glint:tool
{"tool": "write_file", "args": {"path": "hello.txt", "content": "glint was here"}}
>>>
```

and

```
<<<glint:tool
{"tool": "read_file", "args": {"path": "hello.txt"}}
>>>
```

Notice the closing marker is just `>>>`, **not** `>>>glint` as my parser expects. In `src/core/protocol.ts` I currently have:

```ts
export const TOOL_OPEN = "<<<glint:tool";
export const TOOL_CLOSE = ">>>glint";

export interface ToolCall {
  tool: string;
  args: Record<string, unknown>;
  raw: string;
}

const BLOCK_RE = /^[ \t]*<<<glint:tool[ \t]*\r?\n([\s\S]*?)^[ \t]*>>>glint[ \t]*$/gm;
```

Because `TOOL_CLOSE` is set to `">>>glint"` and the regular expression explicitly looks for `>>>glint`, the parser never matches the blocks that end with just `>>>`. Consequently `parseToolCalls` returns an empty array, which is why the earlier debug run showed `=== PARSED CALLS === []`.

The fix is straightforward:
1. Change `TOOL_CLOSE` to match the actual closing marker, i.e. `">>>"`.
2. Update the `BLOCK_RE` pattern so it accepts either `>>>` or `>>>glint`. I can make the trailing `glint` optional with a non‑capturing group: `>>>(?:glint)?`.
3. Keep the rest of the file untouched.

I’ll edit `src/core/protocol.ts` by replacing the exact block that defines `TOOL_OPEN`, `TOOL_CLOSE`, the `ToolCall` interface, and `BLOCK_RE`. I’ll use the edit tool with `replace_all: false` because I only want to replace this specific snippet, not any other occurrences that might coincidentally match.

Here’s the old snippet I’ll replace:

```ts
export const TOOL_OPEN = "<<<glint:tool";
export const TOOL_CLOSE = ">>>glint";

export interface ToolCall {
  tool: string;
  args: Record<string, unknown>;
  raw: string;
}

const BLOCK_RE = /^[ \t]*<<<glint:tool[ \t]*\r?\n([\s\S]*?)^[ \t]*>>>glint[ \t]*$/gm;
```

And the new snippet should be:

```ts
export const TOOL_OPEN = "<<<glint:tool";
export const TOOL_CLOSE = ">>>";

export interface ToolCall {
  tool: string;
  args: Record<string, unknown>;
  raw: string;
}

const BLOCK_RE = /^[ \t]*<<<glint:tool[ \t]*\r?\n([\s\S]*?)^[ \t]*>>>(?:glint)?[ \t]*$/gm;
```

This will allow the parser to correctly capture tool blocks regardless of whether the closing marker includes the optional `glint` suffix. After applying this change, the next run of the headless test should yield proper parsed tool calls, enabling the agent to actually execute the `write_file` and `read_file` actions.

